    "pr_title": "planner, expression: use the range of column types to simplify expressions",
    "pr_body": "<!-- Thank you for contributing to TiDB!\r\n\r\nPR Title Format:\r\n1. pkg [, pkg2, pkg3]: what's changed\r\n2. *: what's changed\r\n\r\n-->\r\n\r\n### What problem does this PR solve?\r\n\r\nIssue Number: close #10497 \r\n\r\nProblem Summary:\r\nEvery integer type has a value range. \r\nFor example: column a is `tinyint` type -> [-128, 127]\r\nWhen there have a selection `a > 200`, the constant 200 is always greater than the maximum value that `tinyint` can achieve. So, the selection `a > 200` always be false.\r\n\r\n### What is changed and how it works?\r\nWhen we compare an integer column to an integer constant. We should use the range of column types to simplify expressions.\r\n\r\n**To easily review this PR, please see the following hints**:\r\n* The changes in files `util/ranger/ranger_test.go` and `planner/core/testdata/plan_suite_out.json` are because of the execution plan in some of the original test cases has become `tableDual`.\r\n* In the file `expression/flag_simplify_test.go` we define functions that contain various integer types for testing. At the same time, some additional columns containing the `not null` attribute is added.\r\n* The input sample in the file `expression/testdata/flag_simplify_in.json` can only focus on results of a certain type. For example, `col1`, `col2`, `col11` are all related columns of `tinyint`.\r\n* The query type basically revolves around the upper and lower bounds of the relevant scope. For example, the range of `tinyint` is [-128, 127], the value of some query conditions will be less than -128 or greater than 127.\r\n* The expected result of most queries is that the execution plan is converted to `tableDual` or `fullTableScan`\r\n\r\n### Check List <!--REMOVE the items that are not applicable-->\r\n\r\nTests <!-- At least one of them must be included. -->\r\n\r\n- Unit test\r\n- Integration test\r\n\r\n### Release note <!-- bugfixes or new feature need a release note -->\r\n\r\n- utilize the range of column types to simplify expressions\r\n",
    "pr_comments": [
        "Related to [PR#20194](https://github.com/pingcap/tidb/pull/20149).",
        "If you want to review this PR easily. Please see the hint in the description. That may help you. Thanks! ",
        "I am concerned that there may be some unveiled corner cases which would produce wrong results since this logic is so trivial. Is it possible to use a simpler approach to deal with this optimization? Just a quick thought, what if we introduce ranger to do this expression simplification? For example, we can build a new filter based on the type of the column, i.e, `a >= -128 and a <= 127`, and put it together with the original filter, e.g, `a > 200`, then let ranger simplify this `a >= -128 and a <= 127 and a > 200` to `false` by using the `point` interfaces. Ranger has already implemented this range merge functionality.",
        "> I am concerned that there may be some unveiled corner cases which would produce wrong results since this logic is so trivial. Is it possible to use a simpler approach to deal with this optimization? Just a quick thought, what if we introduce ranger to do this expression simplification? For example, we can build a new filter based on the type of the column, i.e, `a >= -128 and a <= 127`, and put it together with the original filter, e.g, `a > 200`, then let ranger simplify this `a >= -128 and a <= 127 and a > 200` to `false` by using the `point` interfaces. Ranger has already implemented this range merge functionality.\r\n\r\nGood catch! I will investigate the new idea.",
